#: ../../plugins.rst:1
msgid "Write a native plugin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:4
msgid "A plugin adds a service to Guidedog. A build plugin can transform source text, transform a rendered HTML page, or supply static files. A publisher sends a finished site to a destination. These services use ordinary Odin procedure tables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:8
msgid "This chapter builds a product badge. It replaces ``{{product}}`` in source text, adds a label to HTML pages, and supplies the label's CSS. GitHub Pages then shows how the same plugin foundation supports a service with external effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:14
msgid "Build hooks are available from Guidedog 0.6.2. Earlier 0.6.x releases support publishers only. Use a 0.6.2 host or build it from this checkout before following the badge tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:18
msgid "1. Understand the boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:21
msgid "``plugins.Plugin`` describes the plugin. Its ``source``, ``html`` and ``assets`` procedures are optional. A nil procedure means that service is absent. ``load`` prepares private state; ``unload`` closes resources. The host owns the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:27
msgid "Source hooks precede parsing. HTML hooks and generated assets enter staging. Publication follows a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:27
msgid "Build services precede publication."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:54
msgid "Source hooks run before parsing each primary RST or Markdown document. They apply to PDF and text builds too. Includes remain the engine's responsibility. HTML hooks receive complete pages, including generated indexes and additional pages. Generated assets belong to HTML builders and live below ``_static/plugins/ID/``. The host collects them once during plugin setup, before reading documents. They are fixed for that build; source and HTML hooks cannot add later assets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:61
msgid "A plugin table contains build hooks or a publisher, never both. Normal builds cannot invoke a publishing service. Installed plugins are trusted native programs; registration is explicit and pins the executable's SHA-256. It is not a sandbox."
msgstr ""

