#: ../../plugins.rst:214
msgid "Cleanup accepts zero, failed and already released owners. Allocate returned data with the callback allocator. Check allocation errors; after helpers that hide those errors, check ``plugins.memory_refused(ctx.allocator)``. Return ``plugins.memory()`` when refused. Unload must tolerate a partially loaded session."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:219
msgid "Use ``read_plugin_config`` and ``read_plugin_file`` during load. They fingerprint the bytes actually read and declare dependencies. Keep loaded state unchanged throughout the build. If you read other files yourself, declare their paths in ``session.dependencies`` and append a fingerprint of the loaded values to ``session.cache_key``. Append selected environment values there too. Do not erase the fingerprints recorded by the SDK helpers or depend on undeclared changing data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:226
msgid "The host includes identity, configuration, dependencies, cache key, builder, language and generated assets in cache invalidation. Each build gets private sessions. Reading workers serialize callback access; callbacks need not be thread safe. Native callbacks cannot be interrupted by a process deadline. Installed programs have a deadline per whole request, including pipe writes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:234
msgid "Guidedog loads a plugin and collects HTML assets before reading sources. Hooks run during reading and rendering. Cleanup follows success or failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:234
msgid "Installed build-plugin lifecycle. Asset collection belongs to setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:254
msgid "Text is limited to 8 MiB per hook. Each plugin may supply at most 256 assets and 16 MiB of asset bytes. Paths must be portable and relative. Duplicate names, case-only collisions, traversal and conflicts with project files are refused. ``--untrusted`` disables build plugins. Child-process memory is outside the host's build budget; native plugin code must still check allocations and its own limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:260
msgid "7. Explain failures and test them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:263
msgid "Return a ``Problem`` with a code, title, affected path, explanation, concrete correction and nonzero exit status. For example, ``product = 42`` is invalid; the badge explains that a string is required and suggests ``product = \"Orbit\"``. Markup diagnostics after expansion identify plugin-expanded input and show its actual excerpt. They do not pretend expanded line numbers belong to the original."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:269
msgid "Test the package directly and through its executable. Cover changed settings, unchanged rebuilds, nested links, invalid markup, asset collisions, cancellation, timeouts and allocation refusal. A failed build must preserve the previous site. The repository's build-plugin integration test compiles this exact example and checks HTML, directory HTML, single-page HTML, text and failed generations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:275
msgid "8. Add a publishing service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:278
msgid "GitHub Pages uses the same explicit descriptor foundation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:293
msgid "Its ``Publisher`` supplies ``load``, ``plan``, ``publish``, ``status`` and ``recover``. A session owns configuration and credentials. A snapshot owns checked site files. A plan compares that snapshot with a target baseline. A result reports the verified outcome. Release each owner with its matching SDK procedure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:298
msgid "Follow ``examples/pages-publisher/main.odin`` into ``lib/pages``: configuration and machine binding establish authority; planning reads the target; publishing writes a durable journal before one conditional Git push; status and recovery settle the outcome. Nothing in a source or HTML callback grants publication authority."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:303
msgid "A remote acceptance is not a live deployment. ``Accepted`` and ``Pending`` return 6; an uncertain write returns ``Unknown`` and 7, with status or recovery directions. Never retry an uncertain write blindly. See :doc:`howto/github-pages` for the complete user workflow, and the API reference for :odin:type:`~plugins.Publisher`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:308
msgid "9. Version and distribute the contract"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:311
msgid "Build-plugin API 1.0 uses protocol ``guidedog-build-plugin`` 1.0. Publisher SDK 1.1 keeps ``guidedog-publisher`` protocol 1.0 and accepts SDK 1.0 publishers. These are separate contracts; older hosts reject build plugins rather than treating them as publishers. Product versions are separate from interface versions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:316
msgid "Register each rebuilt executable deliberately. ``guidedog plugin inspect ID`` shows its kind and declared hooks. The public catalog describes plugins; it does not download, install or run them. Custom markup readers and renderers still use the compiled ``core.Registry`` API. Installed custom directives, roles and builders are not part of this build-hook interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:322
msgid "10. Check a plugin before registering it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:325
msgid "``guidedog plugin check PATH --project=DIR --config=FILE`` exercises a build plugin without changing the registry. Registration runs the same checks automatically: two fresh loads, repeated source and HTML results, generated asset validation and cleanup. ``--timeout=SECONDS`` defaults to 60 per process request. Supply a real configuration when the plugin needs one. Relative configuration paths resolve from ``--project``; its default is the current directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:332
msgid "Passing checks demonstrates interoperability, not a proof of safety. Guidedog keeps checking every runtime response and runs installed plugins in separate processes. A crash, bounds failure or stalled process becomes a diagnostic and cannot publish an incomplete generation. Earlier publisher compatibility is no longer a release requirement; qualification checks demonstrate interoperability across process boundaries, while directly linked native callbacks remain trusted code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:339
msgid "Generic checks never invoke a publisher. Qualify publishers with their own Odin tests and an explicitly configured dry run. A plugin that changes the parser, node model or builder itself belongs in a separate design; the modular libraries and source remain available for an application or a fork."
msgstr ""
